  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "If \\(n\\heartsuit m=n^3m^2,\\) what is \\(\\frac{2\\heartsuit 4}{4\\heartsuit 2}?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( \\ \\dfrac{1}{4} \\)",
    "B": "\\( \\ \\dfrac{1}{2} \\)",
    "C": "\\( \\ 1 \\)",
    "D": "\\( \\ 2 \\)",
    "E": "\\( \\ 4 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The rule cubes the first input and squares the second, so the order of the inputs matters. Applying it to each ordered pair gives\n\\[\n2\\heartsuit4=2^3\\cdot4^2=8\\cdot16=128\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n4\\heartsuit2=4^3\\cdot2^2=64\\cdot4=256.\n\\]\nThe denominator is nonzero, so\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\heartsuit4}{4\\heartsuit2}\n=\\frac{128}{256}=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\tfrac12\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
